{"points": [{"heading": "What is a fraction?", "text": "A fraction shows equal parts of a whole. The top number (numerator) tells how many parts you take. The bottom number (denominator) tells how many equal parts the whole is cut into."}, {"heading": "Same amount, different looks", "text": "Equivalent fractions look different but represent the same amount. For example, 1/2 and 2/4 are equivalent. You can see this using bar models of the same size."}, {"heading": "Check equivalence with bar models", "text": "Draw two bar models the same length. Shade the parts for each fraction. If the shaded areas match, the fractions are equivalent."}, {"heading": "Make equivalent fractions", "text": "Multiply both the numerator and denominator by the same number. This creates more equal parts and shades more parts, but the amount stays the same."}, {"heading": "Compare fractions: use bar models", "text": "Draw bar models the same size for two fractions. Shade each one. The bigger shaded area shows the bigger fraction."}, {"heading": "Unlike denominators: order carefully", "text": "When denominators are different, do not just compare the bottom numbers. Always draw bar models or think about the size of each piece."}, {"heading": "Ascending and descending order", "text": "Ascending order: smallest to largest. Descending order: largest to smallest. Use bar models to compare before arranging."}], "formulas": [{"label": "Make an equivalent fraction", "rule": "Multiply the numerator and denominator by the same whole number.", "example": "To make 1/2 equivalent to something with denominator 4: multiply both by 2. Answer: 1×2=2 and 2×2=4, so 1/2 = 2/4."}, {"label": "Check if two fractions are equivalent", "rule": "Draw bar models of the same total length. Shade the parts. If the shaded areas are equal, the fractions are equivalent.", "example": "Is 1/3 equivalent to 2/6? Draw two same-size bars. Shade 1 out of 3 parts in the first. Shade 2 out of 6 parts in the second. The shaded areas match, so yes, they are equivalent."}, {"label": "Compare two fractions with unlike denominators", "rule": "Draw bar models of the same total length. Shade the parts for each fraction. Compare the shaded areas. The bigger shaded area is the bigger fraction.", "example": "Compare 1/2 and 1/4. Draw two bars the same length. Shade 1 out of 2 in the first. Shade 1 out of 4 in the second. The first bar's shaded area is bigger, so 1/2 > 1/4."}, {"label": "Order fractions from smallest to largest", "rule": "Draw bar models of the same length for each fraction. Compare the shaded areas. Arrange them by size.", "example": "Order 1/4, 3/4, and 1/2. Draw three equal-length bars. Shade 1 out of 4, then 3 out of 4, then 1 out of 2. Smallest to largest: 1/4, 1/2, 3/4."}]}
